```c
struct PacketHeader {
    unsigned int status : 3; // 'status' is a bit field 3 bits wide
    // other members
};
```
The colon `:` is used to specify the bit width after the member name. A 3-bit field can store $2^3 = 8$ distinct values (0-7).

An unnamed bit field is used to create padding within a struct to align subsequent members on a specific bit boundary or to reserve space. It cannot be accessed by name.
Example:
```c
struct DeviceRegister {
    unsigned char status : 1;
    unsigned char : 3; // Unnamed bit field, 3 bits of padding
    unsigned char error_code : 4;
};
```
Here, the 3-bit unnamed field ensures `error_code` starts after `status` with a 3-bit gap.

A zero-width bit field forces the next bit field to start on the next allocation unit boundary of its underlying type. If the zero-width bit field itself is the first member, it forces the next member to start at the beginning of the next allocation unit. It's used for explicit alignment.
Example:
```c
struct Example {
    unsigned int a : 5;
    unsigned int : 0; // Forces 'b' to start in a new unsigned int
    unsigned int b : 10;
};
```
In this example, `a` would occupy 5 bits in the first `unsigned int`, and `b` would start in a completely new `unsigned int`, even if there was space remaining in the first one.
